# dot-server-deploy — the environment, for a machine nobody is typing at.
#
#   cp deploy/env.example .env      # then edit
#   set -a; . ./.env; set +a
#   ./server
#
# `.env` is gitignored. This file is the documented example and is committed.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# READ THIS BEFORE UNCOMMENTING ANYTHING IN THE FIRST SECTION
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE CONFIGURATION LIVES IN `cfg/`, NOT HERE. Port, bind address, name, slots,
# tickrate and everything else are YAML: `cfg/net.yml` has `net_port`,
# `cfg/server.yml` has `sv_name` and `sv_maxplayers`. That is the normal place to
# change them and the only place a comment explaining WHY survives.
#
# The layering, which is the family's and is the same in every project here:
#
#     exported defaults  <  cfg/*.yml  <  DOT_SERVER_*  <  command line
#
# The `TMC_*` variables below are NOT a fifth layer. `./server` turns each one
# into the matching command-line argument, so they land in the LAST column and
# outrank the YAML. That is deliberate -- a container has no file to edit and
# needs some way in -- but it means **an uncommented TMC_PORT here makes
# `cfg/net.yml` look broken**: somebody edits the port, restarts, and the server
# keeps binding the old one with nothing anywhere saying why.
#
# So they ship COMMENTED OUT. Uncomment one only when this box genuinely cannot
# carry a `cfg/` (an immutable image, a panel that owns the port allocation), and
# when you do, treat the YAML for that one key as dead.
#
# `DOT_SERVER_*` is the other way in and sits at the correct layer -- BELOW the
# command line, above the file. `DOT_SERVER_SV_MAXPLAYERS=32` is a nudge that a
# `--max-players` still beats. Prefer it over `TMC_*` for anything you want
# overridable at run time.
#
# AND THIS IS ONE SERVER. `demo.sh` is still what stands up the stack -- five
# servers on their own ports behind nginx, each with its own game -- and it sets
# all of this itself, per server. Nothing here is for that; it is for a single
# server on its own box, which is what the Pterodactyl egg beside this file runs.

# --- one server, only if cfg/ cannot be used -------------------------------
# Each maps to the ./server option of the same name; see ./server --help.
# COMMENTED ON PURPOSE. See above.
#TMC_PORT=6070
#TMC_BIND=0.0.0.0
#TMC_NAME=TMC Bhop/Surf Server
#TMC_MAX_PLAYERS=64

# Also in cfg/, as `sv_game` in server.yml -- which is the better place for it.
# The parser has always accepted that key; it was simply not written down in the
# shipped file, so it was invisible unless you read tmc_config.gd.
#TMC_GAME=g2gfast

# Where the YAML is, where the games are, where the server writes. Not in cfg/
# for the obvious reason -- these say where cfg/ IS. Defaults are relative to the
# project and are usually right.
#TMC_CONFIG_DIR=cfg
#TMC_CONTENT_DIR=content
#TMC_DATA_DIR=data

# The Godot runtime. setup.sh bakes the one it found into ./server; this
# overrides it, which is what an image built somewhere else needs.
#TMC_GODOT=/usr/local/bin/godot

#TMC_VERBOSE=1

# --- the exported engine ---------------------------------------------------
# Genuinely environment-only: no part of this is a property of a running server,
# so none of it is in cfg/.
#
# Where the browser client will be SERVED FROM. Baked into web/build/tmc-loader.js
# by `./server export-web` and by `./server stamp-loader`.
#
# It must be an origin of its own, not a path on the site: the loader frames the
# engine in a sandbox that is only a boundary while the frame is cross-origin,
# and refuses to start if this resolves to the page's own origin.
#
# Must be the same string as TMC_S3_PUBLIC_BASE below. When they disagree the game
# loads the PREVIOUS deployment and nothing anywhere reports an error.
TMC_ENGINE_BASE=https://games.example.net/game/

# --- publishing the engine to a bucket yourself ----------------------------
# WHAT TO DO. Credentials are a separate family at the bottom of this file, and
# the split is explained there.
# THIS IS THE STANDALONE PATH -- a bucket you own, serving the engine at a fixed
# URL. If the site's play centre is publishing your builds instead, it uses its
# own GAMES_S3_* credentials and its own bucket, and none of this applies.
TMC_S3_BUCKET=
TMC_S3_REGION=us-east-1
TMC_S3_PREFIX=game/
TMC_S3_PUBLIC_BASE=https://games.example.net/game/

# S3-compatible storage that is not AWS (R2, Spaces, MinIO).
#TMC_S3_ENDPOINT=https://<account>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com

# no-store while the engine is changing under you. Turn it up once a deployment is
# stable -- the bytes are immutable between exports and this is tens of megabytes.
#TMC_S3_CACHE_CONTROL=public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Pre-compress before upload. Pick this OR a CDN that compresses, never both.
#TMC_S3_GZIP=1
# Use `aws s3api` where the CLI is installed, instead of the built-in signing.
#TMC_S3_USE_CLI=1
#TMC_S3_SOURCE=web/build

# --- nginx in front of a game server ---------------------------------------
# An HTTPS page may not open a plain ws:// socket, so a browser client needs this.
#TMC_SERVER_DOMAIN=demo.example.com
#TMC_SERVER_PUBLIC_PORT=6065
#TMC_SERVER_BACKEND=127.0.0.1:6071
#TMC_SERVER_SSL_CERT=/etc/letsencrypt/live/demo.example.com/fullchain.pem
#TMC_SERVER_SSL_KEY=/etc/letsencrypt/live/demo.example.com/privkey.pem

# --- nginx as the game origin, instead of a bucket -------------------------
#TMC_GAME_DOMAIN=games.example.net
#TMC_GAME_WEB_ROOT=/srv/tmc-game
#TMC_GAME_CACHE_CONTROL=no-store
# Who may frame the player. This is the frame-ancestors list, and it is the only
# thing stopping any site on the internet embedding your game as their own.
#TMC_SITE_ORIGINS=https://tmcdevgames.net https://www.tmcdevgames.net

# --- credentials -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHY THESE ARE NOT CALLED TMC_S3_ANYTHING, which is a fair question:
#
#   TMC_S3_*        WHAT TO DO. Bucket, region, prefix, public base, caching.
#                   Ours, so they carry our prefix.
#   AWS_*           WHO YOU ARE. The standard names, which every AWS tool,
#                   CI runner and IAM role already sets -- renaming them would
#                   mean a box that is already authenticated has to be told
#                   again in our dialect.
#   S3_ACCESS_KEY / S3_ACCESS_SECRET
#                   Also who you are, in the WEBSITE's dialect. Accepted as a
#                   fallback purely so one .env can drive this script and
#                   website-city without holding the same secret twice.
#
# Configuration and credentials are separate jobs, which is the whole reason
# they do not share a prefix: one belongs in a deployment's config management,
# the other in its secret store, and a single prefix invites putting both in
# whichever is more convenient.
#
# PRECEDENCE, which nothing else states: AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID beats S3_ACCESS_KEY,
# and AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY beats S3_ACCESS_SECRET. Set both pairs and the AWS
# ones win. AWS_SESSION_TOKEN has no S3_ equivalent -- temporary credentials are
# an AWS concept. TMC_S3_REGION falls back to AWS_DEFAULT_REGION, then us-east-1.
#
# A FOURTH FAMILY EXISTS AND IS NOT READ HERE. website-city writes the games
# bucket with GAMES_S3_REGION / GAMES_S3_BUCKET / GAMES_S3_ACCESS_KEY /
# GAMES_S3_ACCESS_SECRET, which is a DIFFERENT bucket from the one below, with
# different credentials, written by its worker rather than by this box. If the
# site is publishing your builds, none of the TMC_S3_* block above applies.
#
# THERE IS NO RCON PASSWORD HERE, and that is not an omission. DotConfig refuses
# secrets from the environment as well as from argv -- `ps` shows one and
# /proc/<pid>/environ shows the other, and both end up in pasted bug reports. It
# lives in cfg/rcon.yml, which is a file with a mode on it.

# Pick ONE pair. Both are read; the AWS ones win if you set both.
#AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
#AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
#AWS_SESSION_TOKEN=

#S3_ACCESS_KEY=
#S3_ACCESS_SECRET=
